 "margin_locators": [],
 "observations": [
  "body L5: a heavy bracket opens Schiaparelli's addition, which goes on to p. 228.",
  "body L6, L8, L13, L15: the dashes of «al–Battānī», «16–17», «72–83» and «’l–qar.» are long dashes (5.0 to 5.3 pt).",
  "body L8, L11-L12: the middle of the eclipse at 1h 7m 1/2 (p. 226) less an eighth of an hour gives 1h 0m; 3° 16′, the equation of days of Leo 19° in al-Battānī's table (Part II, p. 63), are 13m 4s at 4m to the degree; 1h 0m − 13m 4s = 0h 46m 56s.",
  "body L14-L24: the heads stand over braces; every motion is that of al-Battānī's tables: the year 1191 of the era on Part II, p. 72, 11 years on p. 73, the end of tammūz on p. 74, 8 days on p. 75; the motions in 30, 15 and 2 minutes are a half, a quarter and a thirtieth of those of one hour on p. 76 (2′ 28″, 32′ 56″, 32′ 40″, 8″). The sums are right, and 137° 5′ 42″ + 37° 36′ 50″ = 174° 42′ 32″.",
  "body L25-L27, L30-L31, L33: 140° 53′ 57″ − 82° 25′ = 58° 28′ 57″ and 140° 53′ 57″ − 1° 39′ 37″ = 139° 14′ 20″; al-Battānī's equation of the Sun (Part II, p. 79: 1° 39′ 5″ at 58°, 1° 40′ 9″ at 59°) gives 1° 39′ 36″ for 58° 28′ 57″. The bars before «1 39 37» and «1 5 24» (L33) and between ☾ and ⊙ (L30-L31) are minus signs.",
  "body L28-L31: 137° 5′ 42″ − 140° 53′ 57″ = − 3° 48′ 15″, doubled 7° 36′ 30″; 137° 5′ 42″ + 2° 5′ 48″ = 139° 11′ 30″.",
  "body L29, L32-L34: al-Battānī's correction of the anomaly (Part II, p. 78) is 1° 2′ for a double elongation of 7° and 1° 11′ for 8°, so 1° 7′ 28″ for 7° 36′ 30″; the printed 1° 5′ 24″ is 2′ 4″ less, and 334° 15′ 31″ − 1° 5′ 24″ = 333° 10′ 7″. For 333° 10′ 7″ his simple equation (2° 6′ 43″ at 333°, 2° 2′ 17″ at 334°) gives 2° 5′ 58″, against the printed 2° 5′ 48″; at this elongation the table adds nothing for the distance of the epicycle. 174° 42′ 32″ + 2° 5′ 48″ = 176° 48′ 20″.",
  "body L35-L38: 180° − 176° 48′ 20″ = 3° 11′ 40″; 4′ 8″ is an eighth of the hourly motion in latitude, 32′ 56″ + 8″ = 33′ 4″; for 3° 7′ 32″ al-Battānī's table of the latitude (Part II, p. 78: 0° 15′ 40″ at 3°, 0° 20′ 53″ at 4°) gives 0° 16′ 19″.",
  "notes L1: from 82° 15′ in 1191 the apogee moves to the 82° 25′ of 1202 (L26), 11 years at 1° in 66 years being 10′."
 ],
